                  "itemId": 17592186576136,
                  "kind": "tutorial",
                  "knowledgePoint": null,
                  "markdown": "The derivatives of sine, cosine, and tangent are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin x$ | $\\cos x$ |\n| $\\cos x$ | $-\\sin x$ |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\sec^2 x$ |\n\nNow that we know the derivatives of the standard trigonometric functions, we can differentiate any combination of them by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of $y = \\cos x + \\sin x.$\n\nHere, we can use the fact that the derivative of the sum is equal to the sum of the derivatives, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll derive the derivatives shown in the table at the end of this lesson.",
                  "mathAcademyId": "2618",
                  "requiresCalculator": null,
                  "solution": "",
                  "status": "completed",
                  "stepId": "17592186060528",
                  "title": "Introduction"
                },
                "total": 6
              },
              {
                "number": 2,
                "order": 2,
                "step": {
                  "canContinue": true,
                  "contentId": 17592186059503,
