                  "fields": [],
                  "itemId": 17592186576137,
                  "kind": "example",
                  "knowledgePoint": {
                    "id": 17592186059675,
                    "title": "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"
                  },
                  "markdown": "Find the derivative of $y =  3\\cos x  + 4\\sin x + 2x^3.$",
                  "mathAcademyId": "e-3631",
                  "requiresCalculator": null,
                  "solution": "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3cos⁡x+4sin⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
                  "status": "completed",
                  "stepId": "17592186060529",
                  "title": "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"
                },
                "total": 6
              }
